* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Raw source data is preserved byte for byte -- no line-ending conversion.
#
# The SHA-256s in data/raw/census/PROVENANCE.md are hashes of the files as the
# Census Bureau served them, so that a reviewer can re-download and compare.
# Under the blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above, git normalised CRLF to LF
# in the stored blob: nc-est2024-agesex-res.csv went in at 15,864 bytes against
# the 16,171 that were downloaded. The hash then verifies on Windows, where
# checkout restores CRLF, and fails on Linux, where it does not. A provenance
# hash that depends on the checking platform is worse than none, because it
# fails in a way that looks like tampering.
#
# The WONDER exports are covered for the same reason: their footers are the
# citable artifact and their content hashes appear in `fetched_from` provenance
# strings, so their bytes must not shift underneath them either.
data/raw/census/* -text
data/raw/wonder_exports/* -text
